Resource bindings after a pre-child guard setup failure
=====================================================

This supplement is a new metadata Source subject. It does not modify the
bounded-output Core, numerical workers, science manifests, resource profile,
limits, old combined Source peer, or the 54/72 accepted controls. Those remain
their exact earlier subjects. In particular it is not an entry admitted by the
old Core metadata budget. Its Root-only dispatcher has cooperative 256 MiB,
125 second, 64 MiB read, 512 path, 4 MiB file and 16 MiB fresh-write bounds.
There is no external guard claim for this dispatcher. It never starts a
process, calls a scientific predicate or decodes an Original parent.

The finite failure class is a genuine FileNotFoundError at args.output.mkdir,
before a child or role output exists. It is not a resource-floor refusal. The
old complete SDK/raw stream and the old budget, request, gate, preparation and
launch stay immutable. Root owns genuine absence observations separately.
Fresh v2 SDK/raw names require a new budget, hence a new request/gate and later
launch. Exact parent/import, numerical manifest, role, output and guard paths,
profile, caps and all nonbinding request bytes remain unchanged. Only the two
SDK inventory paths and two literal command reference paths may change in the
budget. Hash/size slots remain symbolic and acyclic.

prepare_retry emits seven fresh documents: budget, request, gate, preparation,
launch, parent IO and positive transition. Only Root may activate the entry.
The request and gate pin the budget; the later launch pins all three. The
parent IO declaration has the inherited eighty-row union, 10-row child subset,
57,540,737-byte content/allocation envelope and 256 MiB reserve. It is explicitly
not a Core launch authority. Existing failed SDK destinations are never reused,
deleted, moved or overwritten. All new targets are fresh. The old import and
old preparation gate are retained as historical original-meaning provenance,
not misrepresented as issuers of the new binding.

The old supplier_preparation remains unchanged. A Root selected-attempt map
continues to name its original preparation and adds resource_binding_transition.
The effective-preparation adapter authenticates both versions and the genuine
old failure before using the exact effective preparation. Its actual record
retains original_prepared, effective_prepared and transition. This is a
different-argv resource-binding transition, not the old identical-argv retry.
Science-attempt history remains separate from metadata-attempt history. Other
terminal suppliers stay v1 and are never replayed.

The dispatcher loads the exact old generic operation modules as charged
borrowed Source. The source identity of a function is retained even when its
documented context/adapters change: new metadata records name this supplement,
and old initialization/preparation API records retain their old program,
packet, peer and Core controls. The original cohort/base packet/source owner
are joined explicitly. New operations have a new Root gate, exact independent
Source peer and fresh changed-path controls. No old peer/control is retagged.

The new bounded_common.py is an explicitly disclosed metadata helper edition:
its acquired cohort directory remains the old absolute directory, and its
Source loader checks fixed lengths before bounded reads. The exact difference
from the physically unchanged old common.py is supplied. Bootstrap packet and
self-Source reads, and late CAS stage/after reads, are also length bounded
